y --days 5\n2292864 python -m benchmarks.recertify --days 5\n32\n 02:09:07 up 15 days,  8:03,  1 user,  load average: 29.74, 34.10, 35.04\n1:# Using machine learning to understand the nature of MOSP — third plan\n2:\n3:*2026-09-27. The sequel to `reports/ml_nature_plan_2.md`. Twenty-seven sections\n4:of `reports/ml_nature.md` and the synthesis `reports/ml_nature_summary.md`\n5:leave seven questions, ranked by what depends on them. This plan turns the\n6:ones that are loop-shaped into items, cuts the ones that can be cut, and is\n7:honest about the two that a loop can only equip: a branching-aware bound and\n8:the Lean equality are proof problems, and the loop supplies their harnesses\n9:and their statements, not their proofs. Nothing here changes a solver default;\n10:every proposed change is measured in nodes behind a flag and left for the\n11:owner.*\n12:\n13:## 0. What the third loop stands on\n14:\n15:- Every refutation at n ≤ 40 is sound on twenty independent searches and\n16:  92% carry a checked DRAT proof; above 40 the only checks are agreement\n17:  between two configurations and among a few relabellings (§15, §17).\n18:- The C `better_move` was fixed on 2026-09-26 (`0eb33915`); the fixed rule\n19:  costs +7% nodes at n ≤ 40, ×2–3.5 at the 100-customer half-ratio classes\n20:  and ≥ 15× on the one ridge instance at 100 with both sides on record (§18).\n21:  Which of the fix's two changes carries that cost is unmeasured.\n22:- Hardness is a property of the labelled customer graph and of nothing else\n23:  in the matrix, for the customer search; the SAT encoding, with a variable\n24:  per product position, was never tested (§13).\n25:- The ridge sits at cover excess `(n_ones − m) / n ≈ 2–2.4` across every\n26:  product ratio, one independent cycle of the incidence graph per customer;\n27:  its rate falls by 0.002 per customer per ten customers from 10 to 75 (§16,\n28:  §25). Nothing at 100 on the ridge is certified beyond a 5-instance sample.\n29:- The bound's blocked family is trees of cliques with branching; `tw + 1` is\n30:  a theorem and beats the bound on 933 instances; no formula over thirty\n31:  invariants beats `max(lb_best, tw + 1)` (§6, §21, §24).\n32:- The two-key closing rule beats every learned policy; whether it is the\n33:  literature's MCNh under another name is open, now that Becceneri, Yanasse &\n34:  Soma (2004) — obtained 2026-09-27 — states MCNh as an arc-traversal\n35:  heuristic (§7, `literature/MISSING.md`).\n36:- `Sandwich.lean` proves both sandwich inequalities; `Reduction.lean` had\n37:  `mosp ≤ pathwidth + 1` with a Hall's-theorem `sorry` and no `≥` (§26) —\n38:  **superseded 2026-09-27 before item 12 ran**: that statement was over the\n39:  pattern graph and false; `MOSPGraph.lean` now proves the equality over the\n40:  MOSP graph, `sorry`-free, both directions. Item 12 is reduced to the three\n41:  tree-decomposition `sorry`s of `Sandwich.lean`.\n42:\n43:## 1. The questions and their items\n44:\n45:**Q6 — two cheap closures.** (01) `learning.corpus_sweep` with the two-key\n46:rule seeding `restricted_dfs`, against `learned+cs-dfs`'s 709 better / 13\n47:worse over `cs-dfs`; decides whether the LightGBM dependency question exists.\n48:(02) Implement the 2004 arc-traversal Minimal Cost Node heuristic from its\n49:pseudocode, verify on the paper's worked example (Table 1, ξ′ = 4, the printed\n50:sequence), compare with Frinhani et al. (2018)'s MCNh column on the Challenge\n51:and SCOOP instances and with our `mcn` and the rule. *Kill for 02:* if the\n52:paper's example cannot be reproduced from the pseudocode, say what is\n53:ambiguous and stop.\n54:\n55:**Q5 — does the graph story hold for SAT?** (03) On §13's re-covering and\n56:relabelling pairs, `decide_mosp` through the SAT path at `optimum − 1` and\n57:`optimum`, conflicts and seconds recorded; paired ratios; predict SAT\n58:conflicts from graph-only against graph-plus-matrix features. *Kill:* SAT\n59:cost as cover-independent as the search's (paired median within ±5%); then\n60:the two procedures cannot fail on disjoint sets for that reason.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7926e52b-a7c9-488a-920f-3bf6526697d2","uuid":"3638fe02-c652-4ccc-a59f-1886e6d7f179","timestamp":"2026-09-28T01:09:07.280Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"Mon Sep 28 02:09:07 AM WEST 2026\n1498439 /bin/bash -c source /home/al/.claude/shell-snapshots/snapshot-bash-1790365714628-r3qcc1.sh 2>/dev/null || true && shopt -u extglob 2>/dev/null || true && { \\builtin unalias -- 'unsetenv'; \\builtin unset -f -- 'unsetenv'; } >/dev/null 2>&1 || true && eval 'cd /home/al/dev/MOSP && git add solutions/Random-125-125-2-5_0.json && git commit -q -m \"Recertify Random-125-125-2-5_0 at 20: 458.4 billion nodes, under the pre-2026-09-26 better_move rule  The refutation ran on the library built before the 0eb33915 fix (the file'\"'\"'s note refers to the 2026-09-23 fix). It is a refutation under a rule since shown to over-prune on 0.3% of sparse instances at n ≤ 40; the value is a verified upper bound and its optimality claim carries that caveat until the instance is re-refuted on the fixed code (reports/ml_nature.md §15, §18).  Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>\" && git add CLAUDE.md literature/MISSING.md literature/becceneri_yanasse_soma_2004_method_mosp_cutting.pdf literature/yanasse_1997a_transformation_pattern_sequencing_wood.pdf reports/ml_nature_plan_3.md Ralph_Loops/loop0004 Ralph_Loops/loop0003/loop.log Ralph_Loops/loop0003/nohup.out Ralph_Loops/loop0003/status.json && git commit -q -m \"Fold loop0003 findings into CLAUDE.md; Yanasse 1997a and Becceneri et al. 2004 obtained; third plan and loop0004  loop0003 finished all fourteen items (reports/ml_nature.md §15–§27, synthesis in reports/ml_nature_summary.md); findings and kill-criteria verdicts folded into CLAUDE.md. Two papers from the author settle the 1997c venue question (it is Pesquisa Operacional 17(1), 57–70) and give MCNh'\"'\"'s 